Additional permission under GNU GPL version 3 section 7
-------------------------------------------------------

As the copyright holder, I give you permission to convey Isomar, or a work based on it, through an
application store or a similar distribution service (such as Apple's App Store, Google Play or F-Droid)
whose terms of service or usage rules restrict how recipients may use, copy or share what they receive
(for example digital rights management, limits on the number of devices, or a ban on further
distribution), even where those restrictions would otherwise conflict with the GPL, on these
conditions:

  1. the Corresponding Source of the exact version conveyed through the store is available to
     everyone, at no charge, under the GPL with this permission, from a place named in the app's
     About page or store listing; and

  2. every other condition of the GPL is met.

This permission covers only the restrictions the store's terms impose on recipients. It does not
change the GPL in any other way.

If you modify Isomar, you may extend this permission to your version of it, but you are not obliged
to do so. If you do not wish to, delete this permission notice from your version.

Contributions
-------------

Contributions to Isomar are accepted under the GPL with this permission, so that it can go on being
granted for the whole program.

Third-party code
----------------

This permission applies only to Isomar's own code. The libraries linked into the phone and tablet
apps keep their own licences: cairo (LGPL-2.1 or MPL-1.1), pango, glib and fribidi (LGPL-2.1 or
later), harfbuzz, fontconfig, pixman, libffi and expat (MIT), freetype (FreeType licence), libpng
(libpng licence), pcre2 (BSD), zlib (zlib licence), and the Rust crates listed in Cargo.lock under
their own terms. The LGPL libraries are linked statically on iOS and Android; their source, and the
build scripts that link them (scripts/deps/build-mobile-deps.sh), are published with Isomar's, so that
a user can rebuild the app against modified versions of them.
